          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been diagnosed with high blood pressure.",
          "answer": "I'm sorry to hear about your diagnosis. Living with a new diagnosis can bring up a lot of questions, so here is what the reference material in this tool says. It is general information, not advice for you personally.\n\n**What high blood pressure is.** Most cases have no single identifiable cause. Factors that contribute include age, genetics, excess body weight, high dietary salt, low potassium intake, physical inactivity, alcohol, poor sleep (including untreated sleep apnea), and chronic stress. High blood pressure often causes no symptoms, which is why it is usually found by measurement rather than by how someone feels. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**How it is classified.** US society guidance sorts adult office readings into normal (under 120/80 mm Hg), elevated (120–129 systolic, under 80 diastolic), stage 1 (130–139 systolic or 80–89 diastolic), and stage 2 (140 systolic or higher, or 90 diastolic or higher). European guidance uses a 140/90 threshold for office hypertension, so the same reading can be labeled differently depending on which guidance is used. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, cardiology society guidance.)\n\n**Targets.** The US guidance cited for people with coronary artery disease sets a target below 130/80 mm Hg for most people. The retrieved passages do not give a target for everyone, so your own target depends on your situation. (Source: *Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention*, cardiology society guidance.)\n\n**Follow-up.** After treatment starts or changes, guidance describes rechecks roughly monthly until the target is reached, then every three to six months once readings are stable. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*.)\n\n**What I could not find.** The search results did not cover which medicines are suitable for you, doses, or how to lower your readings in your specific case. Those decisions belong with your clinician, who knows your full history.\n\n**Questions you might bring to your clinician:**\n- What is my target blood pressure, and how was it chosen for me?\n- Should I check my readings at home, and how often?\n- Do any lifestyle factors, such as salt, alcohol, sleep or weight, apply to me?\n- Could a condition like sleep apnea or kidney disease be contributing?\n- How often will we review my readings?\n\nIf you develop severe headache, chest pain, shortness of breath, confusion, weakness or trouble speaking, or vision changes, seek urgent medical care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
