          "session_id": "full-mt-m-001",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What about diet?",
          "answer": "Diet is one of the main lifestyle measures for high blood pressure. The reference material describes two parts.\n\n**An overall eating pattern.** The pattern most consistently linked to lower blood pressure emphasizes vegetables, fruit, whole grains, low-fat dairy, poultry, fish, beans, nuts and vegetable oils. It limits saturated fat, full-fat dairy, fatty meat, sweets and sugary drinks. The approach works as a whole pattern, not as one \"blood pressure food\" added to an unchanged diet. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**Reducing salt (sodium).** Cutting sodium is listed as a measure that applies to everyone with high blood pressure. Combining a healthier eating pattern with lower sodium gave a larger effect than either alone. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*; *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**What I could not find.** The search results did not give a daily sodium limit, specific foods to add or avoid for you, or how much your readings might change. They also did not say whether any eating plan suits your other health conditions or medicines. Your clinician or a registered dietitian can help you work out a plan you can keep up.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
